<section class="section animate" aria-labelledby="risk-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header section-header--left">
      <span class="title-tag bar-eyebrow" aria-hidden="true">Honesty</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" id="risk-title">111 of them are labelled caution, and we tell you which</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="legal-layout legal-layout--single">
      <div class="legal-prose">
        <p>
          Not every tweak suits every machine. The app carries a risk label on all 525 and shows
          it before you apply anything:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>414 are marked safe.</b> They change a Windows setting with no side effect worth warning you about.</li>
          <li><b>111 are marked caution.</b> They turn off something you might actually use. Disabling Bluetooth services is great on a desktop and wrong on a laptop with wireless headphones. The label is there so you can skip them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The app also rates expected impact, and it is deliberately unglamorous: <b>26 high, 108 medium
          and 391 low</b>. Most single tweaks do very little on their own. The gain comes from the total,
          which is the honest version of a claim this category usually inflates.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every change can be undone. Revert them one at a time, revert a whole category, or roll the
          machine back from a restore point the app can make for you before it touches anything.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/pages/safe">More on safety, anti-cheat and what we do not do</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

